coder › Implemented `parseMoney(text)` on branch `work/parse-money` in two commits.

What changed:
- `src/money.ts` — implemented `parseMoney`: detects exactly one currency (`€`/`EUR`, `$`/`USD`, `£`/`GBP`, code matched case-insensitively with word boundaries, anywhere in text), otherwise throws `TypeError` naming the text. Grabs the first digit run, strips spaces, treats a trailing `,`/`.` + exactly two digits as the decimal separator and every other separator as grouping. Leading `-` (also U+2212) before the first digit, or the whole trimmed text wrapped in `(...)`, negates (except zero stays `0`). No digits throws `TypeError` naming the text. Verified `€1.234,56` → 123456 EUR and `$1,234` → 123400 USD.
- `src/money.test.ts` — new tests covering the two spec examples, symbol/code anywhere, space grouping, whole amounts, minus/parens negatives, and `TypeError` (with the text in the message) for missing/unknown currency and no digits.

Decisions not in the plan: exactly-one-currency required (so mixed `$`+`€` also throws); space is grouping-only, never decimal; parens count only when wrapping the whole trimmed text (accounting style `($10.50)`); trailing minus does not count as "leading".

Checks:
- `bun test`: `8 pass, 0 fail, 19 expect() calls, Ran 8 tests across 1 file.`
- `bunx tsc --noEmit src/money.ts`: clean, no output (first version used `Set`/`includes`/`startsWith` which failed under the default pre-ES2015 lib, so rewrote with `indexOf`/`charAt`/regex trim; re-ran clean).

